[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-84462"] :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-1)^{2}=-5(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-1=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p(x-1) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p & =-5 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=(-\\frac{5}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2(-\\frac{5}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-84850"] :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-2=3t,\\,y+2=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-2}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y+2)}{3} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =6(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
